Course.start({"num":16,"unit":4,"slug":"m16-slope","minutes":35,"calculator":"not needed","title":"Slope","subtitle":"Rise over run, slope from two points, and slope as a rate of change","lede":"Slope measures steepness: of a hill, a wheelchair ramp, a roof, a graph. It's one number that tells you how fast something is changing.","recap":"An arithmetic sequence adds the same amount, d, each time. Its rule, like a(n) = 4n − 1, is a linear function: the points lie on a line.","goals":["Find the slope of a line from a graph using rise over run","Find the slope between two points with the slope formula","Recognize positive, negative, zero and undefined slopes","Interpret slope as a rate of change, with units"],"context":{"title":"The steepest street in the world","place":"Dunedin, New Zealand · 2019 to 2020","text":"**Baldwin Street** in Dunedin, New Zealand, is a short residential street that climbs a hill so steep that tourists come just to walk up it. For years, *Guinness World Records* listed it as the **steepest street in the world**.\n\nThen in **2019**, a man named Gwyn Headley measured a street in his town, **Ffordd Pen Llech** in Harlech, Wales. Guinness agreed it was steeper, and Wales took the title. The people of Dunedin weren't happy.\n\nA New Zealand surveyor went back to Baldwin Street and measured it a new way, along the center of the road where people actually walk. In **April 2020**, Guinness gave the title back: Baldwin Street's steepest section rises about **1 meter for every 2.86 meters** across, a slope of about **35%**.\n\nHow can you compare two hills fairly? With **slope**: how much it goes **up** (rise) for each step **across** (run). For comparison, U.S. law says a wheelchair ramp can rise **at most 1 inch for every 12 inches**, a slope of about 8%.","ask":"Which is steeper: a ramp that rises 1 foot over 12 feet, or one that rises 2 feet over 20 feet?"},"learn":[{"title":"Rise over run","text":"The **slope** of a line is\n\n> {{slope = (rise)/(run)}} = (change in y)/(change in x)\n\nPick two points on the line. Count how far you go **up** (+) or **down** (−) to get from one to the other: that's the **rise**. Count how far **right**: that's the **run**.\n\nIn this graph, from (0, 1) to (2, 4) you rise 3 and run 2, so the slope is {{3/2}}.","fig":"rise","check":{"q":"A line goes **down 4** for every **2 to the right**. Its slope is…","choices":[["{{-2}}",true,"−4 ÷ 2 = −2."],["{{2}}",false,"Going down means the rise is negative."],["{{-1/2}}",false,"Rise goes on top: −4/2."]]}},{"title":"The slope formula","text":"For any two points {{(x₁, y₁)}} and {{(x₂, y₂)}}, the slope {{m}} is\n\n> {{m = (y₂ - y₁)/(x₂ - x₁)}}\n\n**Subtract in the same order** on top and bottom: if y₂ comes first on top, x₂ comes first on the bottom.","worked":[{"problem":"Slope through {{(1, 2)}} and {{(4, 11)}}","steps":[["{{m = (11 - 2)/(4 - 1)}}","Second point minus first point, top and bottom."],["{{= 9/3 = 3}}"]]},{"problem":"Slope through {{(-2, 5)}} and {{(3, -5)}}","steps":[["{{m = (-5 - 5)/(3 - (-2))}}"],["{{= -10/5 = -2}}","Watch the double negative on the bottom: 3 − (−2) = 5."]]}]},{"title":"Four kinds of slope, and slope as a rate","text":"Read a graph from **left to right**, like a sentence:","table":{"head":["Slope","The line…","Example"],"rows":[["positive","goes **up**","{{m = 2}}"],["negative","goes **down**","{{m = -1/3}}"],["zero","is **flat** (horizontal)","{{m = 0}}"],["undefined","is **vertical**: the run is 0, and you can't divide by 0","the line {{x = 4}}"]]},"after":"In a real situation, slope is a **rate of change**, and it has units: **(y units) per (x units)**. If a graph shows distance in miles over time in hours, a slope of 60 means **60 miles per hour**. If it shows savings in dollars over weeks, a slope of 15 means **$15 per week**."}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"quiz","title":"Slope from a graph","questions":[{"q":"What's the slope of this line?","fig":"s1","choices":[["{{2}}",true,"From (0, −1) to (1, 1): rise 2, run 1."],["{{1/2}}",false,"Rise over run: up 2, right 1."],["{{-2}}",false,"The line goes up from left to right."]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"What's the slope of this line?","fig":"s2","choices":[["{{-1/3}}",true,"Down 1 for every 3 right."],["{{-3}}",false,"Rise is −1, run is 3."],["{{1/3}}",false,"It goes down, so the slope is negative."]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"What's the slope of this line?","fig":"s3","choices":[["{{0}}",true,"A flat line never rises."],["undefined",false,"Vertical lines have undefined slope. This one is horizontal."],["{{3}}",false,"3 is where it crosses the y-axis, not its slope."]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"What's the slope of this line?","fig":"s4","choices":[["{{3/4}}",true,"From (−4, −2) to (4, 4): rise 6, run 8, and 6/8 = 3/4."],["{{4/3}}",false,"Rise goes on top."],["{{6}}",false]],"keepOrder":true}],"hints":["Find two points where the line crosses grid corners exactly.","Count the rise (up or down) first, then the run (right)."]},{"id":"t2","type":"solve","title":"Slope from two points","pick":6,"intro":"Use {{m = (y₂ - y₁)/(x₂ - x₁)}}. Type **undefined** if the run is 0.","items":[{"q":"{{(1, 3)}} and {{(3, 7)}}","a":"2","pre":"m =","from":"(7-3)/(3-1)","how":["{{(7 - 3)/(3 - 1) = 4/2}}","**2**"]},{"q":"{{(0, 5)}} and {{(4, -3)}}","a":"-2","pre":"m =","from":"(-3-5)/(4-0)","how":["{{(-3 - 5)/(4 - 0) = -8/4}}","**−2**"]},{"q":"{{(-2, 1)}} and {{(4, 4)}}","a":"1/2","pre":"m =","from":"(4-1)/(4-(-2))","how":["{{(4 - 1)/(4 - (-2)) = 3/6}}","**1/2**"]},{"q":"{{(-3, -2)}} and {{(-1, -8)}}","a":"-3","pre":"m =","from":"(-8-(-2))/(-1-(-3))","how":["{{(-8 - (-2))/(-1 - (-3)) = -6/2}}","**−3**"]},{"q":"{{(2, 6)}} and {{(7, 6)}}","a":"0","pre":"m =","from":"(6-6)/(7-2)","how":["{{0/5 = 0}}: a horizontal line"]},{"q":"{{(5, 1)}} and {{(5, 9)}}","a":"undefined","pre":"m =","how":["{{(9 - 1)/(5 - 5) = 8/0}}","Can't divide by 0: **undefined** (a vertical line)"]},{"q":"{{(-4, 2)}} and {{(2, -2)}}","a":"-2/3","pre":"m =","from":"(-2-2)/(2-(-4))","how":["{{(-2 - 2)/(2 - (-4)) = -4/6}}","**−2/3**"]},{"q":"{{(0, 0)}} and {{(6, 9)}}","a":"3/2","pre":"m =","from":"9/6","how":["{{9/6 = 3/2}}"]}],"hints":["Subtract the y's on top and the x's on the bottom, in the same order.","Subtracting a negative is adding: 4 − (−2) = 6."]},{"id":"t3","type":"sort","title":"What kind of slope?","cats":["Positive","Negative","Zero","Undefined"],"items":[["A line that rises from left to right",0],["{{y = 5}}",2,"Horizontal: y never changes."],["{{x = -2}}",3,"Vertical."],["Through (1, 4) and (3, 0)",1,"y goes down as x goes up."],["Through (−3, 2) and (5, 2)",2,"Same y: flat."],["A ski slope, skiing left to right downhill",1],["Savings that grow $10 a week",0],["Through (4, 1) and (4, 6)",3,"Same x: vertical."]],"hints":["Up from left to right → positive. Down → negative.","Flat → zero. Straight up and down → undefined."]},{"id":"t4","type":"mistake","title":"Slope slip-ups","intro":"Tap the **first wrong line**, then type the correct slope.","items":[{"q":"Slope through {{(2, 1)}} and {{(6, 9)}}","lines":["{{m = (6 - 2)/(9 - 1)}}","{{= 4/8 = 1/2}}"],"bad":0,"why":"The y's go on **top**: {{(9 - 1)/(6 - 2) = 8/4 = 2}}.","a":"2","from":"(9-1)/(6-2)"},{"q":"Slope through {{(1, 5)}} and {{(3, 1)}}","lines":["{{m = (1 - 5)/(1 - 3)}}","{{= -4/(-2) = 2}}"],"bad":0,"why":"The order must match: if 1 − 5 on top (second minus first), then 3 − 1 on the bottom: {{-4/2 = -2}}.","a":"-2","from":"(1-5)/(3-1)"},{"q":"Slope through {{(-1, 4)}} and {{(3, 6)}}","lines":["{{m = (6 - 4)/(3 - 1)}}","{{= 2/2 = 1}}"],"bad":0,"why":"{{3 - (-1) = 4}}, not 2: {{2/4 = 1/2}}.","a":"1/2","from":"2/4"}]},{"id":"t5","type":"solve","title":"Rate of change","intro":"Find the slope, and think about what it means.","items":[{"q":"A car is **40 miles** from home at 1 p.m. and **190 miles** from home at 4 p.m. What's its average speed (miles per hour)?","a":"50","post":"miles per hour","from":"(190-40)/(4-1)","how":["{{(190 - 40)/(4 - 1) = 150/3}}","**50 mph**"]},{"q":"A phone's battery is at **90%** at 2:00 and **54%** at 5:00. What's the rate of change (percent per hour)?","a":"-12","post":"% per hour","from":"(54-90)/(5-2)","how":["{{(54 - 90)/(5 - 2) = -36/3}}","**−12% per hour** (negative: it's draining)"]},{"q":"A plant was **6 cm** tall on day 4 and **15 cm** on day 10. How fast did it grow (cm per day)?","a":"1.5","post":"cm per day","from":"(15-6)/(10-4)","how":["{{9/6 = 1.5}}"]},{"q":"A wheelchair ramp may rise **at most 1 inch per 12 inches**. A ramp rises **30 inches**. What's the **shortest** run it can have, in inches?","a":"360","post":"inches","eq":"30/r = 1/12","how":["{{30/r = 1/12}}","{{r = 360}} inches (30 feet!)"]}],"hints":["Rate of change = change in y ÷ change in x.","A negative rate means the quantity is decreasing."]},{"id":"t6","type":"select","title":"Steeper or not?","items":["A slope of {{4}} is {steeper than|less steep than|the same as} a slope of {{2}}.","A slope of {{-5}} is {steeper than|less steep than} a slope of {{3}}. (Compare how far from 0.)","Baldwin Street (rise 1, run 2.86) is {steeper than|less steep than} a wheelchair ramp (rise 1, run 12).","A line with slope {{1/10}} is {almost flat|almost vertical}.","A line with slope {{100}} is {almost vertical|almost flat}."],"hints":["Steepness depends on how far the slope is from 0, in either direction."]}],"build":{"heading":"Measure a slope","prompt":"Find a slope in real life: a ramp, a staircase, a driveway, a hill, a roof. **Estimate** or measure its rise and run (for stairs, one step's height is the rise and its depth is the run). Calculate the slope as a fraction **and** a decimal, and compare it to Baldwin Street (about 0.35) or a wheelchair ramp (about 0.08).","model":"I measured one stair at school. The rise is 7 inches and the run is 11 inches, so the slope is 7/11 ≈ 0.64. That's almost twice as steep as Baldwin Street (0.35), which makes sense because stairs are much steeper than any street. It's about 8 times steeper than a wheelchair ramp.","placeholder":"I measured …","reqs":[{"t":"Give the rise and the run","re":"\\b(rise|run)\\b","min":2},{"t":"Write the slope as a fraction","re":"\\d+\\.?\\d*\\s*/\\s*\\d+"},{"t":"Write it as a decimal","re":"\\d*\\.\\d+"},{"t":"Compare (use *steeper*, *less steep* or *flatter*)","re":"\\b(steeper|less steep|flatter|not as steep)"}]},"reflect":{"q":"Dunedin didn't give up when it lost the title. It re-measured more carefully and got it back. When have you had to look at something again more carefully (a test answer, a disagreement, a project) and found you'd been right, or wrong?","placeholder":"Once I looked again and…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Slope puzzles","items":[{"q":"A line through {{(2, 3)}} and {{(6, y)}} has slope {{5/2}}. What is {{y}}?","a":"13","pre":"y =","eq":"(y - 3)/(6 - 2) = 5/2","how":["{{(y - 3)/4 = 5/2}}","{{y - 3 = 10}}, **y = 13**"]},{"q":"A line through {{(x, 1)}} and {{(4, 7)}} has slope {{-3}}. What is {{x}}?","a":"6","pre":"x =","eq":"(7 - 1)/(4 - x) = -3","how":["{{6/(4 - x) = -3}}","{{6 = -12 + 3x}}, so {{3x = 18}}, **x = 6**"]},{"q":"Baldwin Street's steepest part rises 1 m for every 2.86 m of run. Express the slope as a percent, to the nearest whole percent.","a":"35","post":"%","how":["{{1/2.86 ≈ 0.3497}}","**35%**"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"quiz","title":"Lines and points","questions":[{"q":"Are {{(0, 1)}}, {{(2, 5)}} and {{(5, 11)}} on the same line?","choices":[["Yes: the slope between each pair is 2",true,"(5−1)/2 = 2 and (11−5)/3 = 2."],["No",false,"Check the slope between each pair of points."]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"A line has slope 0 and passes through {{(3, -4)}}. What's its equation?","choices":[["{{y = -4}}",true,"Horizontal: every point has y = −4."],["{{x = 3}}",false,"That's vertical (undefined slope)."],["{{y = 3}}",false]]},{"q":"Two lines both pass through the origin. One has slope 3 and the other slope −3. They are…","choices":[["mirror images across the y-axis",true,"Flipping x to −x changes the sign of the slope."],["the same line",false],["parallel",false,"Parallel lines never meet, but these both pass through the origin."]]}]}],"figs":{"rise":{"x":[-1,5],"y":[-1,6],"w":240,"lines":[{"fn":"1.5x + 1"}],"points":[[0,1,"(0, 1)"],[2,4,"(2, 4)"]],"segs":[[0,1,2,1,2,true],[2,1,2,4,2,true]],"texts":[[1,0.5,"run 2"],[2.9,2.5,"rise 3"]],"alt":"A line through (0, 1) and (2, 4) with a run of 2 and a rise of 3 marked"},"s1":{"x":[-4,4],"y":[-5,5],"w":200,"lines":[{"fn":"2x - 1"}],"points":[[0,-1],[1,1]],"alt":"A line through (0, −1) and (1, 1)"},"s2":{"x":[-6,6],"y":[-4,4],"w":220,"lines":[{"fn":"-x/3 + 1"}],"points":[[0,1],[3,0]],"alt":"A line through (0, 1) and (3, 0)"},"s3":{"x":[-5,5],"y":[-2,5],"w":200,"lines":[{"fn":"3"}],"alt":"A horizontal line at y = 3"},"s4":{"x":[-5,5],"y":[-4,5],"w":220,"lines":[{"fn":"0.75x + 1"}],"points":[[-4,-2],[4,4]],"alt":"A line through (−4, −2) and (4, 4)"}},"culture":"**Did you know?** In the U.S., road signs give a hill's slope as a percent grade. A 6% grade sign warns truck drivers that the road drops 6 feet for every 100 feet. Baldwin Street, at about 35%, would get quite a sign.","unitTitle":"Linear Functions","code":"MDg0My1OSVdETEFC","star":"MDcxNC1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m15-sequences.html","next":"m17-slope-intercept.html"});
